#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:5.5.t34-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:5.5.t63
REASONING=The sentence has two coordinated independent verbs, **donarunt** and **existimarunt**, joined by the *et* at t50 (that *et* therefore links the two verbs, not adjacent nouns). Inside the second clause sits a relative clause *qui aliquid de ingeniis poterant iudicare*, whose verb is **poterant** (dependent, introduced by the relative pronoun *qui*, whose antecedent is *omnes*); *iudicare* is a complementary infinitive completing *poterant* and so is not its own verbal expression, though it keeps its own object *aliquid* and the adverbial phrase *de ingeniis*. The four ethnic nouns are coordinated in the "et A et B et C" polysyndetic shape: the first *et* (t36) has only relatedtoken1 -> Tarentini; each subsequent *et* links the preceding and following item. All four are subjects of *donarunt*. The enclitic *-que* (t46) is attached to *ceteris* but functionally joins the two ablatives *civitate* and *praemiis* (with *ceteris* adjectival to *praemiis*). *atque* (t59) joins *cognitione* and *hospitio*, both ablatives governed by the adjective *dignum*. One genuine close call: *cognitione atque hospitio dignum existimarunt* could be analysed as an indirect statement with an elided infinitive *esse* (adding an 'implied sum' token), since *existimo* often takes acc. + inf. I chose instead the object-complement reading, treating *dignum* as a predicate complement related directly to *existimarunt*, because there is no expressed accusative subject here at all (the object is carried over from *hunc* in the first clause), so positing an implied *esse* would require positing an implied accusative object as well, for which the scheme provides no token type. *itaque* is treated as an adverbial connective on *donarunt*.

